</table>

<h2>The settled framework for successive applications</h2>
<p>The Court anchored its reasoning in <strong>Ajwar v. Waseem</strong>, (2024) 10 SCC 768, on the factors governing bail in serious offences, and <strong>State of Karnataka v. Sri Darshan</strong>, (2026) 8 SCC 223, for the proposition that prolonged incarceration does not by itself generate an entitlement to bail. The line of authority quoted within them — Kalyan Chandra Sarkar, Prasanta Kumar Sarkar, Neeru Yadav, Mahipal, Amarmani Tripathi — converges on one discipline: a successive application must identify what has changed since the last refusal. Here the Court found none: <em>&ldquo;I do not consider this to be a fit case for the grant of regular bail.&rdquo;</em></p>

<h2>Practical lessons for the defence</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Time a successive application to an actual development.</strong> A hostile material witness, a demolished recovery, a co-accused&rsquo;s acquittal or a medical emergency each constitute a change; the calendar alone does not.</p>
  <p><strong>Address the earlier orders squarely.</strong> Courts read the history first. An application that does not explain why the previous reasoning no longer holds invites the &ldquo;no material change&rdquo; finding.</p>
  <p><strong>Near the end of evidence, consider the trial court route.</strong> When a handful of witnesses remain, pressing for expedition — day-to-day trial under Section 346 BNSS — may serve the client better than a fresh bail attempt that consolidates adverse findings.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">The Court recorded that its observations are confined to the disposal of this bail application and carry no bearing on the merits of the ongoing trial. Case details are as recorded in the order dated 17 November 2026.</div>
